THE MOON CATS, BY JANE R. CARTER
The Moon Cats is an illustrated children’s book which tells the story of a group of neighborhood cats who, every night, gather together in an alleyway and fly from Earth to the Moon. Once on the Moon, the cats dance and frolic in the moonlight until an evil-looking stranger appears carrying a glowing stone and promising to grant each of the cats their fondest wish. A number of the cats step forward, each making a wish:
• One wishes to sing more sweetly, like the birds they and their human hear in the garden each morning;
• Another wishes to stay forever with their human, able to provide a lifetime of comfort and love;
• Another wishes to be able to talk to, and be understood by, their human;
• Another wishes to have longer and more luxurious hair, to better keep their human warm and cozy at night.
Once all the wishes are made, the stranger raises the stone, and grants each cat’s wish. Unfortunately, the results are not really what the cats would have wanted:
• One cat is transformed into a songbird;
• Another is transformed into a teddy bear;
• Another is transformed into a child;
• And another is transformed into a fur coat.
Seeing this, one of the oldest and wisest of the cats - which knew better than to accept wishes from strangers - leaps at the stranger and claws the stone away from them. Then wrapping itself around the stone, the old cat offers to care for and protect the stone if the wishes could be undone. In a flash, the transformed cats are returned to their former selves and the old cat is changed into something not unlike a flying jellyfish - which carries away both the stone and the evil stranger.
The remaining cats resume their frolic, singing their gratitude to the old cat’s sacrifice which saved them.
